{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_gy69bFHkmii2lZ5mMkfut1zH","content":"1390\tReading `yang/php.cljc` confirms the audit: it has no cells (assignment is\n1391\tlexical rebinding, so `$x = 1; $f = function() use ($x) {...}; $x = 2` and\n1392\tevery loop-carried mutation are wrong except the single-threaded-variable\n1393\tspecial case `for-threaded-var`), no arrays, no strings beyond literals, no\n1394\t`echo` or output, no classes, references, null semantics or coercion, a\n1395\tZ-combinator per function and per loop, type hints parsed and dropped, `-10;`\n1396\tfolded into a literal, and PHP `null` lowered to host `nil`. Its tokenizer has\n1397\tno modes (no HTML, no heredoc, no `<?=`). Replacing only its parser would keep\n1398\tevery semantic defect. **Ruling: replace.** Nothing of the legacy lowering is\n1399\treused; the Z-combinator encoding is superseded by self-applied `:loop`-marked\n1400\tlambdas with cells, which also makes safepoints and tail marks work.\n1401\t\n1402\tMigration mechanics (the F2 pattern):\n1403\t\n1404\t- The ANTLR frontend is installed in `yin.repl.frontends/standard` as a\n1405\t  new revision of `:yang/php` (the compiler frontend keeps its revision\n1406\t  during the migration window; `[id revision]` pins either), with no\n1407\t  fallback.\n1408\t- Every program in `test/yang/php_test.clj` is re-expressed in the new corpus,\n1409\t  with expectations corrected where the legacy was wrong (`null` is\n1410\t  `:php/null`, not `nil`).\n1411\t- The browser demo `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/compilation_pipeline.cljs`\n1412\t  compiles editable PHP synchronously in CLJS with the legacy frontend, while\n1413\t  the local ANTLR parser is JVM-only until a Dart helper exists (amended\n1414\t  2026-10-17: PHP has Java and JavaScript helpers upstream; per-target helper\n1415\t  availability decides the host matrix, `yang.antlr.md` 4.2). The demo moves to **checked-in CST packets**\n1416\t  for its sample programs (generated by a JVM script, the `dht-import-v1.edn`\n1417\t  pattern), lowered and run parserless in the browser; editable PHP source in\n1418\t  the demo is dropped, or routed to a configured parser service when one\n1419\t  exists (owner decision B8.9). `src/dev/subsystems.edn` is a one-line\n1420\t  retarget.\n1421\t- The compiler revision of `:yang/php` retires at P8 (implementation\n1422\t  deleted, no shim; the repository has no backward-compatibility\n1423\t  obligation) once the demo retarget has landed and the corpus covers the\n1424\t  compiler's programs (owner decision B8.6).\n1425\t\n1426\tWhat stays refused after migration: `goto`; return by reference (corner C7);\n1427\t`declare(encoding)`; `__halt_compiler`; `eval` until its slice; `$$` and\n1428\t`extract` until the dynamic-scope slice; extension surfaces (`ext/*`: PDO,\n1429\tcurl, mbstring, gd, intl, openssl, sockets, `pcntl` beyond signals), each a\n1430\tbound surface per ADR 0004; `preg_*` until a portable regex engine exists (a\n1431\tPCRE-compatible engine is large and host engines diverge); `date`, `time`,\n1432\t`microtime`, `rand`, `random_int` and `uniqid` exist only as effects with\n1433\texplicit outcomes.\n1434\t\n1435\t## 6. The grammar route and parser worker\n1436\t\n1437\t- **Package**: `antlr/php/manifest.edn`, from grammars-v4 `php`\n1438\t  (`PhpLexer.g4`, `PhpParser.g4`, MIT), vendored verbatim. The one Java\n1439\t  helper, `Java/PhpLexerBase.java` (modes HTML, Script, CSS, PHP, Heredoc;\n1440\t  `<?=`; heredoc and nowdoc end detection; string-interpolation lexing),\n1441\t  carries an explicit MIT notice (Kochurkin and Marianne) and is **vendored**\n1442\t  beside the `.g4` files with its notice; it does not share the TypeScript\n1443\t  helpers' unresolved-notice situation.\n1444\t- `PhpParser.g4` has no superClass and no predicates, so the parser half is"}
